  <h2>Decide What the Roof Needs to Do</h2>
  <p>Louvres are adjustable blades. On many systems they rotate to change light and ventilation while the frame stays in place. A rotating roof does not necessarily retract out of view. If an open sky is important, check exactly what remains overhead when the roof is fully open.</p>
  <p>Observe the patio at the time you use it, including the lower sun later in the day. A roof may shade the table at lunch yet leave the western seats exposed at dinner. Also consider the effect on daylight and winter sun in adjacent rooms. The Australian Government's <a href="https://www.yourhome.gov.au/passive-design/shading" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Your Home shading guidance</a> explains why orientation, season and climate need to shape the solution.</p>
  <p>Compare the permanent structure with the alternatives in our <a href="/guides/outdoor-shade-setup-for-patios-and-backyards-what-to-check-before-buying/">outdoor shade guide</a>. An umbrella may suit occasional shade; a retractable fabric awning may suit a clear patio floor and an unobstructed sky when packed away. Both still need suitable support and operating limits. A permanent roof is a larger commitment when the furniture layout or use of the space is likely to change.</p>

  <h2 id="measure-the-space">Measure Three Different Spaces</h2>
  <p>A size in the product name is a starting point. Obtain a dimensioned drawing for the exact model, generation, size and freestanding or wall-mounted version. Then mark these three spaces on the ground and against the house:</p>
  <ol class="feature-list">
    <li><strong>The complete footprint:</strong> outside edges of beams, posts and base plates, plus any required foundation or installation space.</li>
    <li><strong>The usable space:</strong> clear distance between posts, clear height under beams and the position of any middle posts, handles or screen tracks.</li>
    <li><strong>The movement space:</strong> the highest point reached by open blades, door and window swings, screen movement and room to reach controls and drainage outlets.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Put the table and chairs inside the clear dimensions, pull the chairs out and walk past them. A base plate on the main route can matter more than a few extra centimetres of roof. Check the <a href="/guides/outdoor-dining-area-setup-what-to-measure-before-buying-furniture/">outdoor dining layout</a> before choosing the next size up.</p>
  <p>For an attached system, include eaves, gutters, doors, downpipes and the available wall height in the survey. Check delivery access separately: the longest beam or heaviest package has to reach the assembly area without being forced around an impossible corner.</p>

  <h2 id="engineering-and-wind">Ask What the Wind Claim Actually Covers</h2>
  <p>A headline wind speed is not enough to decide whether a structure suits your home. The useful evidence must connect the proposed configuration to its installation conditions. Ask the supplier for the current engineering or test documentation, then have the relevant qualified professional confirm its application to your site.</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>Which exact models, spans, sizes and mounting arrangements are covered?</li>
    <li>Which standard, test method and document revision support the claim?</li>
    <li>Does it describe normal operation, a closed or open roof condition, or a structural design limit?</li>
    <li>What site exposure, terrain and other assumptions apply?</li>
    <li>Which foundations, anchors and connections are required?</li>
    <li>Are screens, glass panels or other accessories included in that assessment?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not convert a weather description or a rating from another product generation into a safe operating instruction. If a brochure, manual and warranty give different answers, obtain a written reconciliation before buying. A safety instruction to leave the blades in a particular position also needs to be practical when nobody is home or the power is out.</p>
  <p>There is no model wind-speed comparison in this guide: a number without the matching configuration and supporting evidence would imply more certainty than it provides. The <a href="https://www.qbcc.qld.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/guide-ncbp-awning-sunshade-canopy-blind.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">QBCC guidance on awnings and shade structures</a> is a useful starting point for understanding product suitability and documentation in Queensland; it is not a national approval for an individual kit.</p>

  <h2>Resolve the Foundations Before Ordering the Kit</h2>
  <p>A level surface is useful for assembly, but it does not establish structural capacity. Pavers and tiles tell you little about what lies beneath them. Deck boards are not a substitute for an assessment of the supporting framing and its connections.</p>
  <p>Ask for a design that deals with the whole route by which forces reach the ground: roof, beams, posts, connections and foundations. For a wall-mounted pergola, that includes the existing building and the proposed attachment. The <a href="https://www.qbcc.qld.gov.au/sites/default/files/2021-10/guide-wind-tie-down-connections.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">wind tie-down guide hosted by QBCC</a> explains this connection principle for structures attached to timber-framed homes. It is background guidance, not an anchor schedule for your pergola.</p>
  <p>Before accepting an installation quote, establish who checks the existing slab or deck, who specifies the footings and anchors, and what happens if the site is unsuitable. Ask whether investigation, excavation, concrete, curing time, reinstatement and concealed services are included. A packet of bolts or a general assembly diagram does not settle those site questions.</p>

  <h2>Check Approvals for the Actual Property</h2>
  <p>“Freestanding”, “DIY” and “small” do not establish an exemption. The proposed dimensions, height, location, attachment, property constraints and local rules all matter. Give your council and the relevant building approval authority or certifier the actual drawings before paying a deposit.</p>
  <p>Planning permission and building approval can be separate questions. <a href="https://www.qbcc.qld.gov.au/news/council-certifier-who-approves-what" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">QBCC explains the council and certifier roles in Queensland</a>. In NSW, the <a href="https://www.planning.nsw.gov.au/the-planning-system/development-assessment/planning-approval-pathways/development-types/balconies-decks-and-patios" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official page for balconies, decks, patios and pergolas</a> sets out conditions for exempt development. Meeting one size condition does not mean every condition is met, and NSW rules should not be transferred to another state.</p>
  <p>Confirm which rules apply to your property and proposed work, including any strata or landlord permission. If the design changes later, such as adding side enclosures, check whether the earlier assessment still covers it. Keep the written advice and approval documents with the final drawings.</p>

  <h2>Compare Manual and Motorised Operation</h2>
  <p class="table-hint">Swipe across the table on a small screen to read every column.</p>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Manual and motorised roof comparison" tabindex="0">
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <caption>Questions that affect everyday use</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Choice</th><th scope="col">What it may offer</th><th scope="col">What to verify</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Manual roof</th><td>Adjustment without a roof motor or app.</td><td>Handle position, reach, effort, removable-handle storage and access around furniture.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Motorised roof</th><td>Easier frequent adjustment from a remote or wall control.</td><td>Power supply, controls, motor access, failure behaviour and replacement parts.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Weather sensors or connected controls</th><td>Automation within the system's stated capabilities.</td><td>What triggers movement, priorities between sensors, connectivity needs and behaviour during an outage.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Ask for a demonstration of the controls and the documented response to a power failure. Do not assume a motorised roof has a manual override. Keep children and fingers away from moving blades, and follow the exact operating instructions. Arrange electrical work through an appropriately licensed electrician where required; establish the connection method and cost in the quote.</p>
  <p>A motor, lights or an app can improve convenience. They do not prove that the frame, anchors or drainage suit the site. Likewise, a wind sensor is a control aid, not a guarantee that a retractable awning can remain extended in a storm.</p>

  <h2 id="pergolux-series-4">Pergolux Series 4: Compare the Three Models</h2>
  <p>These are manufacturer-documented features, not a ranking or a test of installed performance. AHC has an affiliate relationship with Pergolux, but these models are not yet approved for affiliate shopping placements because site-specific engineering and other required product checks remain unresolved.</p>

  <div class="pergola-model-grid" aria-label="Pergolux Series 4 model differences">
    <section class="pergola-model">
