  <p class="pergola-source-note"><strong>Manufacturer feature sources, checked 4 November 2026:</strong>
    <a href="https://pergolux.au/products/pergola" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pergola 4 specifications</a>,
    <a href="https://pergolux.au/products/pergola-pro" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pergola 4 Pro specifications</a> and
    <a href="https://pergolux.au/products/pergola-pro-max" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pergola 4 Pro Max specifications</a>.
    These are supporting manufacturer references, not tracked affiliate purchase links or AHC recommendations.</p>
  <p><strong>Before choosing a model:</strong> request the exact size and mounting drawings, check delivery to your postcode, and have the engineering, foundations, anchors, drainage and any electrical work assessed for your property. Manufacturer wind figures remain inconsistent and have not been verified for the relevant configurations, so none is presented here as a usable wind rating.</p>

  <h2>Follow the Rainwater All the Way Out</h2>
  <p>Ask the installer to show where water travels from the closed blades into gutters, through any posts and out onto or away from the site. Check the discharge points against doors, paths, furniture and neighbouring property. Establish who designs and installs any required stormwater connection.</p>
  <p>Request the system's rain-performance evidence and its limits. Check wind-driven rain at open sides, overflow behaviour, seals at the house and whether retained water can drip when blades reopen. Plan for the actual exposure rather than treating the space as an indoor room.</p>
  <p>Also ask how leaves and debris are removed safely, which parts need inspection and how a blocked outlet is accessed. Our <a href="/guides/gutter-maintenance-guide/">gutter maintenance guide</a> helps frame the access and maintenance questions. Keep cushions and items that must remain dry in suitable storage until the shelter's real limitations are understood.</p>

  <h2>Compare the Complete Installed Quote</h2>
  <p>Two kit prices can describe very different finished projects. Ask each supplier to price the same written brief, with GST, inclusions and exclusions clearly stated.</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><strong>Product:</strong> exact model and size, mounting version, colour, controls and accessories.</li>
    <li><strong>Delivery:</strong> postcode charges, unloading, access restrictions, package handling and storage before installation.</li>
    <li><strong>Design and approvals:</strong> survey, engineering, applications, inspections and who arranges each one.</li>
    <li><strong>Site work:</strong> foundations, fixings, attachment assessment, drainage, electrical work and repairs to existing finishes.</li>
    <li><strong>Installation:</strong> assembly, lifting equipment, commissioning, waste removal and handover.</li>
    <li><strong>Ongoing ownership:</strong> cleaning access, servicing, replacement seals, controls, motors and call-out charges.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If the manufacturer introduces an independent installer, establish who contracts with you and who handles workmanship issues. A referral or a no-cost quote is not an included installation service. Verify the contractor's relevant licence and insurance through the appropriate local registers and request a written scope before work begins.</p>

  <h2>Read the Warranty by Component</h2>
  <p>A long headline warranty may apply to the structural frame while the coating, moving mechanisms, seals, motor and electronics have different terms. Make a component list and check each period, exclusion and claim process. Ask who pays for access, labour, removal, freight and reinstallation if a component needs replacement.</p>
  <p>Keep the dated offer, invoice, model details, final drawings, installation records and any required registration. If the sales page and warranty document disagree, obtain the terms that apply to your purchase in writing.</p>
  <p>The <a href="https://www.accc.gov.au/consumers/buying-products-and-services/warranties" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ACCC explains that warranties are additional to Australian Consumer Law guarantees</a>. Consumer guarantees apply automatically and may last longer than a stated warranty. A voluntary warranty's registration or maintenance conditions do not remove those statutory rights.</p>

  <h3 id="pergolux-warranty">Pergolux: What the Warranty Pages Agree On—and Where They Differ</h3>
  <p>Pergolux's <a href="https://pergolux.au/pages/10-year-warranty" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">dedicated Australian warranty policy</a> advertises 10 years for the aluminium structure, 5 years for the coating and 2 years for moving and electrical parts. Its FAQ says those terms apply across its structures. The headline therefore does not mean every component has ten years of cover.</p>
  <p>However, the policy, the Pro product page linked above and the policy's <a href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0644/4777/4878/files/AU_-_PERGOLUX_-_LIMITED_WARRANTY.pdf?v=1756684356" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">full Australian warranty PDF</a> contain different conditions. These were present when checked on 4 November 2026:</p>
  <p><strong>Check which document covers a new order.</strong> The linked warranty PDF has a January 2025 effective date and names the Pergola, Sundream and Skydance families; it does not expressly identify the 2026 Series 4 Pro or Pro Max. The current warranty webpage also refers to purchases through pergoluxshop.au, rather than the current pergolux.au storefront. Ask Pergolux to confirm the applicable dated terms in writing before ordering; the older PDF's model load tables should not be treated as Series 4 engineering evidence.</p>
  <p class="table-hint">Swipe across the table on a small screen to read every column.</p>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Pergolux warranty discrepancies" tabindex="0">
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <caption>Published terms to clarify for the exact purchase and model</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Issue</th><th scope="col">Dedicated policy page</th><th scope="col">Other published wording</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Moving parts</th><td>2 years.</td><td>The Pro product page gives opening and closing mechanisms 5 years.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Installation deadline</th><td>Within 6 months of purchase.</td><td>The Pro product page excludes installation more than 1 year after purchase.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Claim processing</th><td>Within 10 days of receipt.</td><td>The PDF specifies 180 days in clause 3.8.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Reporting a defect</th><td>Within 7 days in its claim instructions.</td><td>The PDF specifies 30 days from discovering the defect in clause 3.1.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The differences may reflect documents updated at different times, but Pergolux has not confirmed that explanation to AHC. We cannot determine from these pages which conflicting provision applies to a new order. Ask for the applicable dated terms in writing, covering the exact model, component periods, installation and registration deadlines, claim process and labour or reinstallation costs. Keep that response with the order.</p>
  <p>This is a documentation discrepancy, not evidence by itself of an unsafe product. The warranty does not replace an assessment of the proposed installation, and the Australian Consumer Law rights explained above remain separate.</p>

  <h2>Use Owner Reviews for the Questions They Can Answer</h2>
  <p>Separate comments about ordering and delivery from accounts of an installed roof after several seasons. Look for the exact generation, size, installation type, location and ownership period. A review of an older manual system cannot establish the reliability of a newly released motorised one.</p>
  <p>Repeated descriptions of a problem can suggest questions to investigate, but a review is not an engineering test or a measured failure rate. Ask how a reported issue was resolved, whether parts were available and who paid the labour. Manufacturer testimonials and independent reviews are both incomplete views of the ownership experience.</p>

  <h2 id="supplier-checklist">Take This Brief to the Supplier and Installer</h2>
  <p>Before committing, bring a marked-up plan of the furniture, routes, house openings and proposed post positions. Ask for these five items to accompany the quote:</p>
  <ol class="feature-list">
    <li>The exact product and configuration drawings, including base plates and open-blade height.</li>
    <li>Engineering and operating documentation applicable to that configuration and the assessed site.</li>
    <li>A foundation, fixing, drainage and electrical scope with named responsibilities.</li>
    <li>The approval pathway, complete installed price and delivery and installation schedule.</li>
    <li>The component warranty, spare-parts arrangements and maintenance and handover documents.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If an essential item remains unanswered, keep comparing or postpone the purchase. A roof that fits the site, the furniture and the ownership routine is more useful than one selected from its largest advertised number.</p>

